# The scaffold

This repo was made from [duckviz-scaffold]({{ scaffold_repo }}), release **{{ kit_ref }}**. The scaffold gives every
app of this kind the same architecture, commands, docs and skills, and brings new capabilities to all of them.
`copier update` changes this file: write this app's differences in docs/deviations.md.

## What comes from where

| Kind | Where | How it changes |
|---|---|---|
| **Shared code** | the `duckkit` package (a dependency in pyproject.toml, at the same release) | only in the scaffold; `copier update` moves the version |
| **Template-managed files** | the list below | `copier update` merges the scaffold's changes in, keeping this repo's edits |
| **This app's files** | everything else: `src/{{ package }}/`, the contract, the pages, the domain skills, most docs | never touched by an update |

Template-managed files:
- `CLAUDE.md` (this app's rules are in `.claude/project-rules.md`, which it imports)
- `pyproject.toml` (keep this app's dependencies; the merge keeps them)
- `.gitignore`, `.python-version`, `.streamlit/config.toml`
- `.github/workflows/tests.yml`, `.github/workflows/scaffold-update.yml`
- `.githooks/pre-commit`
{%- if cloud_run %}
- `Dockerfile`, `.dockerignore`, `.gcloudignore`, `docs/hosting.md`
{%- endif %}
- `docs/scaffold.md`, `docs/cli.md`{% if motherduck %}, `docs/claude-chat.md`{% endif %}
- `tests/app/test_contract_only.py`
- the skills: `app`, `app-check`, `catch-up`, `scaffold-update`{% if motherduck %}, `publish`{% endif %}{% if backup %}, `backup`{% endif %}{% if cloud_run %}, `hosting`{% endif %}

The answers this repo was made with (its name, port, and options) are in `.copier-answers.yml`.

## Options

Every app has the scaffold's capabilities: the contract and its guard, `status`, `sql`, `doctor`, `privacy-check`
and the pre-commit hook, `app --tailnet`, settings in `config.toml`. These options are where apps differ:

| Option | On here | What it adds |
|---|---|---|
| `motherduck` | {{ 'yes' if motherduck else 'no' }} | `publish [--dry-run] [--check]`, `/publish`, docs/claude-chat.md (asking the data in claude.ai) |
| `cloud_run` | {{ 'yes' if cloud_run else 'no' }} | Dockerfile, `/hosting`, docs/hosting.md, the idle pause |
| `backup` | {{ 'yes' if backup else 'no' }} | `backup`, `backups`, `restore`, `/backup` |
| `demo_data` | {{ 'yes' if demo_data else 'no' }} | `demo` builds `data/demo.duckdb` (`src/{{ package }}/demo.py`), `app --demo` reads it, `/demo-data` |

To turn one on: `uvx copier update --trust -d <option>=true`, then review the diff like any update.

## Updating

`/scaffold-update` (or the weekly `scaffold-update` workflow, which opens a PR) brings in a newer release:
1. `uvx copier update --trust` re-renders the template at the old and the new release with this repo's answers, and
   applies the difference here as a 3-way merge. This repo's edits stay. A clash shows as conflict markers.
2. The duckkit version in pyproject.toml moves with it; `uv lock` picks it up.
3. The tests, ruff and `uv run {{ project_name }} doctor` must pass.

The scaffold's CHANGELOG says what each release adds and any steps to take by hand.

## A new capability

When you build something here that every app of this kind could use, build it in the scaffold instead:
1. In the scaffold repo, `/upstream` takes the change: code into duckkit (with tests), files into the template
   (behind a flag if not every app wants it), and a CHANGELOG entry. The scaffold's CI renders and tests sample apps.
2. A release (a `vX.Y.Z` tag) publishes it.
3. Each app gets it with `/scaffold-update`.
